{{smaller block|cooking-stove. {{right|''R. G. White'', Words and their Uses, p. 232.}}}}

'''washy''' (wosh′i), ''a.'' [< ''wash'' + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Watery; damp; moist; soft: as, “the ''washy'' ooze,” ''Milton'', P. L., vii. 303.—'''2.''' Too much diluted; weak; thin: as, ''washy'' tea.
{{smaller block|Meats of a ''washy'' and fluid nature, that slip through the stomach and tarry not for concoction, do no more feed a man’s health than almost if he lived on air.
{{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, I. 432.}}}}
Hence—'''3.''' Wanting in solidity, substantialness, strength, stamina, or the like; feeble; worthless.
{{smaller block|Alas! our women are but ''washy'' toys.
{{right|''Dryden'', Epil. to the King and Queen (1682).}}
''Washy'' he is, perhaps not over-sound.
{{right|''Prior'', Daphne and Apollo.}}}}

'''wasp''' (wosp), ''n.'' [Also dial. ''waps'', ''wops'' (and ''wop''); < ME. ''waspe'', < AS. ''wæsp'', ''wæps'', found also in the form ''wæfs'' in an early gloss, = D. ''wesp'' = MLG. ''wespe'' = OHG. ''wefsa'', MHG. ''wefse'', wasp (cf. MHG. ''wespe'', ''vespe'', G. ''wespe'', Dan. ''vespe'', a wasp, < L.), = L. ''vespa'', a wasp, = Lith. ''wapsa'', a gadfly, horsefly, = Russ. ''osa'', a wasp (cf. OF. ''guespe'', F. ''guêpe'', < MHG. ''wespe''); with formative ''-s'', perhaps < √ ''wap'', sting (cf. E. ''wap''<sup>1</sup>, strike). The word has appar. nothing to do with Gr. σφήξ, a wasp (with which

