readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|These are also called ''paper-wasps'', from the character of their nests, and include the various species of ''Vespa'' known as ''hornets''. See, besides the family names, ''Agenia, Ammophila, Odynerus, Polistes, Sphecius'', etc., ''dauber'' (''e''), ''mud-dauber'', also ''digger-wasp, potter-wasp, sand-wasp, spider-wasp, wood-wasp'', with numerous cuts.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Ther is no ''waspe'' in this werlde that will wilfullok[e]r styngen,
For stappyng on a too of a styncande frere!</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman’s Crede'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 648.}}
<poem>Meanwhile the troops beneath Patroclus’ care
Invade the Trojans, and commence the war.
As ''wasps'', provok’d by children in their play,
Pour from their mansions by the broad highway.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Iliad, xvi. 314.}}}}
'''2.''' Figuratively, a person characterized by ill nature, petulance, peevishness, irritability, or petty malignity.
{{smaller block|Come, come, you ''wasp''; i’ faith, you are too angry.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., ii. 1. 210.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Golden wasp.''' Same as ''goldwasp''.—'''Great-tailed wasp,''' ''Urocerus'' (or ''Sirex'') ''gigas''.—'''Northern wasp,''' ''Vespa borealis''.—'''Tailed wasps,''' the ''Siricidæ'' or ''Uroceridæ'' (which see).—'''Wasp’s-nest boil,''' a sort of carbuncle situated on the nape of the neck, usually only in people of advanced years.}}

'''wasp-bee''' (wosp′bē), ''n.'' A cuckoo-bee; any bee of the genus ''Nomada''.

'''wasp-beetle''' (wosp′bē″tl), ''n.'' A beetle of the genus ''Clytus'', as the British ''C. arietis'', or of a related longicorn genus, as the American ''Cyllene pictus'': so called from their wasp-like maculation.

'''wasp-fly''' (wosp′flī), ''n.'' A British syrphid fly, ''Chrysotoxum fasciolatum'', spotted with yellow on a black ground, and thus somewhat resem-

readings.c3s2:
bling a hornet.

'''wasp-grub''' (wosp′grub), ''n.'' The larva of a wasp, used for bait by anglers. [Eng.]

'''waspish''' (wos′pish), ''a.'' [< ''wasp'' + ''-ish''<sup>1</sup>.] Like a wasp in any way. {{smaller|(''a'') Having a very slender waist, like the petiole of a wasp’s abdomen; wasp-waisted; tight-laced. (''b'') Quick to resent any trifle, injury, or affront; snappish; petulant; irritable; irascible.}}
{{smaller block|In aige [they be] sone testie, very ''waspishe'', and alwaies ouer miserable.
{{right|''Ascham'', The Scholemaster, p. 33.}}
<poem>Ah! thou knowest not
What sting this ''waspish'' fortune pricks me with.</poem>
{{right|''Randolph'', Amyntas, ii. 2.}}}}

'''waspish-headed†''' (wos′pish-hed″ed), ''a.'' Irritable; passionate.
{{smaller block|Her ''waspish-headed'' son has broke his arrows.
{{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, iv. 1. 99.}}}}

'''waspishly''' (wos′pish-li), ''adv.'' In a waspish manner; so as to be like a wasp in any respect.
{{smaller block|He answered rather ''waspishly''—“Why should you bring me into the matter?”
{{right|''George Eliot'', Middlemarch, li.}}}}

'''waspishness''' (wos′pish-nes), ''n.'' Waspish character or state.

'''wasp-kite''' (wosp′kīt), ''n.'' The honey-buzzard or bee-hawk, ''Pernis apivorus.'' See cut under ''Pernis.''

'''wasp-tongued†''' (wosp′tungd), ''a.'' Petulant-tongued; shrewish.
{{smaller block|<poem>Why, what a ''wasp-tongued'' [var. ''wasp-stung''] and impatient fool
Art thou!</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., i. 3. 236.}}}}

